   "newText": "- Use zero-based grid indexing when generating the line coordinate.\n\n## Additional verified instance (2026-09-30)\nThis stream instance confirms the rule for a vertical barrier and both an already-adjacent and translating shape:\n- Demo 1: 30x29, background 9, barrier color 2 at column 12; color-6 shape was already immediately to its right (starting at column 13). It stayed in place and output added color 8 down the full grid height at column 15, just beyond the shape's far/right edge.\n- Demo 2: 17x19, background 4, barrier color 2 at column 14; irregular color-5 shape lay to its left with a two-column gap. It translated right by 2 columns until its rightmost edge was at column 13, adjacent to the barrier, then added color 8 down the full height at column 7, just beyond the translated shape's leftmost edge.\n- Test: 20x19, background 9, barrier at column 13; irregular color-7 shape occupied rows 9-17 and columns 1-4, with rightmost edge initially 9 columns left of the barrier. It translated right by 8 columns, placing its rightmost edge at column 12, immediately adjacent to the barrier. The correct output preserves the pattern at rows 9-17, columns 9-12 and adds a full-height color-8 line at column 8 (one column beyond the translated shape's leftmost edge). This was confirmed CORRECT.\n- Useful calculation: for a vertical barrier at column `b` and object edge on its left at `rmax`, translate by `b - 1 - rmax`; put the full-height 8-line at translated `cmin - 1`. If the object is on the barrier's right, translate left until `cmin = b + 1`, then put the line at `cmax + 1`. For horizontal barriers, apply the analogous row formulas.\n- Avoid the tempting but incorrect shortcut of putting the 8-line at the grid's outermost column: it marks the object's trailing edge, not the canvas edge."
